# boot -- system boot milestone
# Default boot target. Brings up the base system (system-init) and every
# demo workload (all-services). Deliberately does NOT depend on the
# interactive tty service — boot STARTED is the moment the OnBootReady
# hook fires slinit's system-up + rc.local + ifup wrappers, and running
# tty INSIDE that dep chain means bash's login prompt would print
# concurrently with those wrappers, interleaving on the console. tty is
# ordered "after: boot" instead, so the hooks + rc.local complete
# cleanly, then bash prompts on its own line.
type = internal
depends-on: system-init
depends-on: all-services
# tty is started but NOT depended on: waits-for triggers its start
# eligibility, tty's own `after: boot` gates its bring-up until this
# service is STARTED. Effect: boot fires OnBootReady → hooks.Run →
# ifup → rc.local completes → then bash prompt appears cleanly.
waits-for: tty
# trigger-test is a `triggered` service. trigger-autofire is a one-shot
# scripted service that runs `slinitctl trigger trigger-test` right after
# system-init so the demo boots with trigger-test in STARTED instead of
# leaving it hanging in STARTING. The triggered-service pattern is still
# demonstrable — stop / start / trigger it by hand to see the state.
waits-for: trigger-autofire
# hello-initd is auto-detected from /etc/init.d at runtime;
# slinit-check (offline) doesn't scan init.d, so it's not listed here.
# Start it manually in the demo with: rc-service hello-initd start
